msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2051
msgid "server-confirmed"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2052
#: src/Admin/Pages/FormsPage.php:407
msgid "Abandoned"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2052
msgid "<strong>sessions</strong> that started and did not succeed"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2053
msgid "A <strong>completion rate above 100% is not an error.</strong> It means confirmed submissions outnumbered observed starts — which happens when visitors submit with JavaScript blocked. The browser-observed columns are undercounting, and clamping the number to 100 would hide the one figure that tells you so."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2054
msgid "<strong>Which name a form is listed under.</strong> A form collects several labels — its form plugin's own title on confirmed submissions, and what the browser read from the markup (<code>data-cvmtry-form-name</code>, the <code>name</code> or <code>id</code> attribute, or the generic <code>form</code>). The report prefers a real name over a placeholder, the form plugin's title over the markup, and a readable name over a slug, then the label seen on more successes, more often, more recently. Stored events keep their labels; only the choice among them is made this way."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2055
msgid "<strong>Abandonment has a grace period.</strong> A form started ninety seconds ago is being filled in, not abandoned. A start counts as abandoned only after <strong>30 minutes</strong> pass with no confirmed submission after it — in the same causal order funnels use, so a confirmation stored before the browser's own batch still counts; anything more recent shows as <em>still in progress</em>. Without that, abandonment would spike toward 100% for the most recent hour of any window and then decay — an artifact that looks exactly like a real problem. The 30 minutes matches the tracker's session idle window."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2056
msgid "<strong>The latest start is the one judged.</strong> Starts are counted per session. A visitor who submits, comes back, starts again and leaves has abandoned that second attempt, even though they also converted, so one session can show as both a success and an abandonment. A visitor who leaves a first attempt and finishes a later one has abandoned nothing."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2057
msgid "<strong>Friction points.</strong> Where a provider uses native browser validation, Convermetry records which field failed and why:"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2066
msgid "<strong>No value a visitor typed is ever recorded.</strong> A validation event is rebuilt on the server from exactly three whitelisted pieces — the field's developer-chosen id, its type, and which <code>ValidityState</code> flag failed — and every other key in the request is discarded <em>by construction</em> rather than by a blocklist. Field ids are character-restricted and truncated to 64 characters, so an implementation that mistakenly sent a typed value would be stripped to something unrecognizable rather than quietly stored."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2067
msgid "<strong>Elementor is excluded from form-level engagement.</strong> It identifies a form by its display <em>name</em> on the server while exposing a widget <em>id</em> in the browser, so the two cannot be matched reliably — and an engagement figure attributed to the wrong form is worse than none. Elementor submissions are recorded, attributed, delivered and reported normally everywhere else. The other six providers are fully supported."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2071
msgid "Lead status & value"
msgstr ""

