#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:373
msgid "Conversion goals"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:375
msgid "Match tracked activity against configured goals"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:376
msgid "Goals turn ordinary activity — a phone number tapped, a PDF opened, a booking link followed — into counted conversions. Matching runs on the server as events arrive, so turning this off removes that work entirely. Existing goal completions are kept either way; switching it off pauses collection rather than erasing history. Manage goals under <strong>Convermetry → Goals</strong>."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:377
msgid "Logged-in users"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:379
msgid "Exclude logged-in users from tracking"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:380
msgid "Recommended, so admin and editor activity does not skew visitor analytics. Webhook delivery of form submissions is unaffected."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:383
msgid "Browser privacy signals"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:385
msgid "Honor Do Not Track / Global Privacy Control"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:386
msgid "Visitors whose browser sends these signals are not tracked at all — no analytics events are recorded and no analytics context accompanies their form submissions. Enabling this typically reduces recorded traffic. Note DNT/GPC is an opt-out signal, not a consent mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:387
msgid "IP addresses"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:389
msgid "Store visitor IP addresses"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:390
msgid "On by default. Records the visitor's IP with <strong>every analytics event</strong> (page views, clicks, hovers, scroll milestones, conversions) and with <strong>every server-confirmed form submission</strong> — surfaced in analytics reports and sent as <code>form_submission.ip_address</code> in webhook payloads. Useful for fraud checks, spam review, and CRM deduplication. Turning this off leaves the address empty on new rows; rows already stored are unchanged and age out with the retention window. No IP is ever sent to a geolocation service. Behind a proxy or CDN, map the real address with the <code>convermetry_client_ip</code> filter."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:391
msgid "<strong>Privacy note:</strong> in the EU/UK an IP address is personal data. Storing it for general visitor activity — not just leads someone actively submitted — usually needs to be disclosed in your privacy policy and to rest on a lawful basis. When <em>Honor Do Not Track / Global Privacy Control</em> is enabled above, a visitor sending either signal gets no stored IP on either path — their analytics events are not recorded at all, and a form they submit is still delivered but carries an empty address."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:408
msgid "Data"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:411
msgid "Retention period (days)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:413
msgid "Analytics events, form submission records, goal completions, lead status history, and activity log entries older than this are deleted by a daily cleanup job. Default is 90 days."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:416
msgid "Lead value currency"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:418
msgid "Three-letter ISO 4217 code (USD, EUR, GBP, AUD…) used when you record a value against a lead on the Submissions screen. The code is <strong>saved onto each lead</strong> at the moment you enter its value, so changing this later never rewrites what is already recorded — and reports total each currency separately rather than adding different currencies together."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:421
msgid "Activity Log privacy"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:423
msgid "Store form submission data in the Activity Log"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/SettingsPage.php:424
msgid "When disabled, the Activity Log records every delivery's metadata but replaces the visitor's field values, IP address and page URL parameters, and the endpoint's response, with a placeholder — useful when compliance rules forbid a second copy of lead data. The response goes because a receiver can echo back what it was sent. The payload actually delivered to endpoints is unaffected."
